    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d80">

                <pb facs="0214" n="142" sameAs="frus:frus1882/pg142"/>

                <head>No. 80.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Frelinghuysen to Mr.
                    Young</hi>.</head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1882-07-03"><hi rendition="#italic">July</hi> 3,
                        1882</date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 17.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Mr. Holcombe’s dispatch of the 6th of May
                    last, in relation to the residence of American missionaries in the interior of
                    China, has been received. His exposition of the rights of citizens of the United
                    States in China in the note which he addressed to Mr. Carrow, on that date, is
                    regarded as a very clear and discreet statement of what may properly be claimed
                    under the stipulations of existing treaties for our citizens, residents in
                    China, whether engaged in the calling of missionaries or in any other pursuit,
                    and meets with the unqualified approval of the department.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>FRED’K T. FRELINGHUYSEN.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
